1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89lisabeth_de_Bourbon_%281614%E2%80%931664%29 '''Wikipedia....''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth de Bourbon
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Élisabeth de Bourbon (1614–1664))
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth
2 CONT
2 CONT Duchess of Neumours
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Charles Amadeus, Duke of Nemours
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Jeanne, Duchess of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Françoise, Queen of Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT Full name
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth de Bourbon
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	César de Bourbon
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Françoise de Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	August 1614
2 CONT
2 CONT Paris, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	19 May 1664
2 CONT
2 CONT Paris, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth de Bourbon (August 1614 – 19 May 1664) was a granddaughter of Henry IV, King of France and an ancestor of many European Royals. She is an ancestor of Prince Henri the Count of Paris, Orléanist pretender to the French thr
2 CONC one, are among the male line descendants and heirs of Philippe. He is also an ancestor of Juan Carlos I of Spain, of Albert II, King of the Belgians, of Henri, Grand Duke of Luxembourg, and of Vittorio Emanuele, Prince of Naples, 
2 CONC a pretender to the Italian throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Ancestry
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth was born in Paris. Her father was César de Bourbon, duc de Vendôme, legitimised son of King Henry IV of France and his official mistress Gabrielle d'Estrées. Her mother was Françoise de Lorraine (1592–1669), daughter and
2 CONC  heiress of Philippe Emmanuel, Duke of Mercoeur, who was a rival of Henry IV. Her mother was the Duchess of Mercoeur and Penthièvre in her own right and was the greatest heiress of her time.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was styled as Mademoiselle de Vendôme prior to marriage. She was the youngest of three children; she had two older brothers, the Frondeur François de Bourbon, Duke of Beaufort and Louis de Bourbon, Duke of Vendôme whose wife L
2 CONC aura was a niece of Cardinal Mazarin. They were the parents of the notorious débauché Louis Joseph de Bourbon.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 11 July 1643 at the Louvre, Mademoiselle de Vendôme married Prince Charles Amadeus of Savoy, son of Prince Henry of Savoy and Duke of Nemours. They had two daughters who would marry the heirs to the thrones of Savoy and Portuga
2 CONC l, and three sons who died shortly after birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1652, Élisabeth's husband the Duke of Nemours was killed by her brother François de Bourbon, Duke of Beaufort in a duel. Élisabeth herself died in Paris. She managed to secure the income of the lands of Nemours for her two daug
2 CONC hters, but the titles were inherited by other members of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth was an ancestor of both Louis XV of France and Victor Amadeus II of Sardinia, and thus of the Royal Houses of France and Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Princess Marie Jeanne Baptiste of Savoy, Mademoiselle de Nemours (1644–1724), who married Charles Emmanuel of Savoy, ruler of Piedmont in 1665.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Stillborn daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Princess Marie Françoise Élisabeth of Savoy, Mademoiselle d'Aumale' (1646–1683), who married king Alphonso VI of Portugal, in 1666 and afterwards Afonso's younger brother, Pedro, Duke of Beja, regent of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Prince Joseph of Savoy (1649–1649)
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Prince Francis of Savoy (1650–1650)
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. Prince Charles Amadeus of Savoy (1651–1651)
2 CONT
2 CONT This page was last modified on 3 August 2010 at 16:17.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth de Bourbon-Vendôme
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Elisabeth de Bourbon-Vendome)
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth de Bourbon-Vendôme (August 1614 – 19 May 1664), was a granddaughter of Henry IV, King of France and an ancestor of many European Royals.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth was born in Paris. Her father was César de Bourbon, duc de Vendôme, legitimized son of King Henry IV of France and his official mistress Gabrielle d'Estrées. Her mother was Françoise de Lorraine, Duchesse de Mercoeur et 
2 CONC de Penthièvre (1592 - 1669), daughter and heiress of Philippe Emmanuel, Duke of Mercoeur, who was a rival of Henry IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 11 July 1643 at the Louvre, Mademoiselle de Vendôme married Charles Amadeus of Savoy, 6th Duke of Nemours.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had two daughters who would marry the heirs to the thrones of Piedmont and Portugal, and three sons who died shortly after birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie-Jeanne-Baptiste de Nemours (1644-1724), who married Charles Emmanuel of Savoy, ruler of Piedmont in 1665.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie-Françoise de Nemours (1646-1683), who married king Alphonso VI of Portugal, in 1666 and afterwards Afonso's younger brother, Pedro, Duke of Beja, regent of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph (1649 - 1649)
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis (1650 - 1650)
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Amadeus (1651 - 1651)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1652, Elisabeth's husband the Duke of Nemours was killed by her brother François de Vendôme, duc de Beaufort in a duel. Elisabeth herself died in Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth was an ancestor of both Louis XV of France and Victor Amadeus II of Sardinia, and thus of the Royal Houses of France and Italy.
