1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00007192&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louise_Marie_Th%C3%A9r%C3%A8se_d%27Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise Marie Thérèse d'Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise Marie Thérèse
2 CONT
2 CONT Regent of the Duchy of Parma and Piacenza
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise Marie Thérèse d'Artois, Duchess of Parma, around 1840
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenure 	27 March 1854 – 9 June 1859
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Charles III, Duke of Parma
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Margherita, Duchess of Madrid
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I, Duke of Parma
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice, Grand Duchess of Tuscany
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, Count of Bardi
2 CONT
2 CONT Full name
2 CONT
2 CONT French: Louise Marie Thérèse d'Artois
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Charles Ferdinand, Duke of Berry
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Princess Caroline of Naples
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	21 September 1819(1819-09-21)
2 CONT
2 CONT Élysée Palace, Paris, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	1 February 1864 (aged 44)
2 CONT
2 CONT Palazzo Giustinian, Venice, Lombardy-Venetia, Austrian Empire
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	Kostanjevica Monastery, Nova Gorica, Slovenia
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise Marie Thérèse d'Artois (Louise Marie Thérèse; 21 September 1819 – 1 February 1864) was a duchess and later a regent of Parma. She was the eldest daughter of Charles Ferdinand, Duke of Berry, younger son of King Charles X of
2 CONC  France and his wife Princess Carolina of Naples and Sicily, daughter of King Francis I of the Two Sicilies.
2 CONT
2 CONT As the granddaughter of the king, Louise was a petite-fille de France. Her younger brother, Henri, Duke of Bordeaux, was King of France and of Navarre from 2 to 9 August 1830, and afterwards the Legitimist Pretender to the throne 
2 CONC of France from 1844 to 1883.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Ancestry
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 Titles, styles, honours and arms
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 4.1 Titles and styles
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 Titles
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 6 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise's father died when she was five months old. When her grandfather abdicated in 1830, Louise joined the rest of her immediate family in exile, eventually settling in Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 10 November 1845, at Schloss Frohsdorf in Austria, Louise married Ferdinando Carlo, Hereditary Prince of Lucca, known as Charles III, Duke of Parma and Piacenza after 1849. On 17 December 1847 Empress Marie Louise died and her 
2 CONC father-in-law succeeded as Duke Charles II of Parma. The Duchy of Lucca was incorporated in the Grand Duchy of Tuscany, and she and her husband ceased being Hereditary Prince of Lucca becoming instead Hereditary Prince of Parma.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father-in-law was the Duke of Parma for a few months. In March 1848 revolution broke out in Parma supported by King Charles Albert of Sardinia. Ferdinando Carlo escaped from Parma, but was taken prisoner at Cremona. He remaine
2 CONC d a prisoner at Milan for several months until the British government negotiated his release. After a brief sojourn on the island of Malta, he travelled to Naples and then Livorno where he was joined by Louise Marie Thérèse who ha
2 CONC d just given birth to their first son. Then the family sought refuge in England and Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In August 1848 the Austrian army entered Parma, and officially restored Charles II. Ferdinando Carlo and his family, however, remained in England, since hostilities continued between the Austrian and Piedmontese armies. For severa
2 CONC l years Charles II had considered abdicating in favour of Ferdinando Carlo, but he delayed in the hope that when he did so things would be more secure for his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 24 March 1849 the abdication of Charles II was announced. Ferdinando Carlo, still living in England, succeeded to the title of Duke of Parma, Piacenza, and the Annexed States taking the name Charles III. On 18 May 1849 her husb
2 CONC and re-entered Parma, but left again two days later. He did not take over the administration of the duchy until 25 August.
2 CONT
2 CONT When her husband died in 1854, Louise served as Regent for their young son, the new duke Robert I. Like the other rulers of the Central Italian states, she and her son were ousted during the Franco-Austrian War of 1859, and they r
2 CONC etired to Austrian protection in Venice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Various schemes following the war either for her and her son's restoration in Parma, or territorial swaps which might leave them ruling over Tuscany, Modena, or the Romagna, came to nothing, as the whole of central Italy was annex
2 CONC ed by Piedmont in March 1860. Louise lived out the remainder of her life in exile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise died on 1 February 1864 at the age of forty-four in the Palazzo Giustinian in Venice. She was buried in her grandfather Charles X's crypt at the Franciscan monastery Kostanjevica in Goritz, Austria (now Nova Gorica, Sloveni
2 CONC a).
2 CONT
2 CONT Other members of the French Royal Family buried there include her brother Henri, Count of Chambord, her aunt Marie Thérèse of France, and her uncle Louis Antoine, Duke of Angoulême.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinando Carlo and Louise had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Margherita Maria Theresa Enrichetta of Parma (1847-1893)
2 CONT
2 CONT     ∞ Carlos, Duke of Madrid, Carlist claimant to the Spanish throne, had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Roberto Carlo Luigi Maria, Duke of Parma (1848-1907)
2 CONT
2 CONT     ∞ Princess Maria Pia of the Two Sicilies (daughter of King Ferdinand II of the Two Sicilies and Maria Theresa of Austria), had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT     ∞ Infanta Maria Antonia of Portugal (daughter of King Miguel of Portugal and Adelaide of Löwenstein-Wertheim-Rosenberg), had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Alicia Maria Carolina Ferdinanda Rachael Giovanna Filomena of Parma (1849-1935)
2 CONT
2 CONT     ∞ Ferdinand IV, Grand Duke of Tuscany, had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Enrico Carlo Luigi Giorgio, Count of Bardi (1851-1905)
2 CONT
2 CONT     ∞ Princess Maria Luisa Immaculata of the Two Sicilies (daughter of King Ferdinand II of the Two Sicilies and Maria Theresa of Austria).
2 CONT
2 CONT     ∞ Infanta Adelgundes of Portugal, Duchess of Guimarães (daughter of King Miguel of Portugal and Adelaide of Löwenstein-Wertheim-Rosenberg).
2 CONT
