1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Susanne de Charette Van Stockum Submitted by Brigadier General R. R. Van Stockum, USMC (Ret.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Susanne de Charette Van Stockum, wife of Brigadier General Ronald R. Van Stockum, U. S. Marine Corps, (Retired), died on May 20 [2000] at Jewish Hospital Shelbyville, Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Van Stockum, daughter of Marquis and Marquise de Charette, was born in Paris, France on April 12, 1915. Her mother, Susanne Henning, was the daughter of James Williamson Henning of Louisville and Sue T. Henning of Shelby Coun
2 CONC ty, Kentucky. She was the granddaughter of General Baron Athanase de Charette, leader of the Papal Zouaves in Rome and hero of the Franco-Prussian War of 1870, and Antoinette Polk, Baronne de Charette, a heroine of the American Ci
2 CONC vil War and niece of Bishop General Leonidas Polk. She was a direct descendant of Charles X of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Van Stockum was the owner of Allen Dale, a historic farm in Shelby County, Kentucky, established in 1795 by Robert Polk Allen, son of John and Ann Polk Allen from Frederick County, Virginia. John Allen's descendants have owne
2 CONC d Allen Dale continuously since its establishment. During the period 1907-28, her grandmother Sue Henning, a direct descendant of John and Ann Allen, was owner and breeder of a prize-winning herd of registered Jersey cattle at All
2 CONC en Dale. In 1914 the farm produced the national grand champion Jersey bull. Under the direction of Henning, who became the first woman to serve on the board of the American Jersey Cattle Club, Allen Dale Farm became nationally and
2 CONC  internationally renowned.
2 CONT
2 CONT Susanne visited Shelby County as a young child, staying with her grandmother Henning at Allen Dale and developing a great fondness for the farm and for her grandmother whom she affectionately called "Dovie."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1949 she married then Lieutenant Colonel Van Stockum who was stationed in New York. She and her husband celebrated their Fiftieth Anniversary in June of last year. She is survived by her husband and their three children, Michel
2 CONC e Solange Myers of Palm Beach, Florida, Ronald R. Van Stockum, Jr. of Shelby County, and Charles Antoine Van Stockum of Prospect, Kentucky, and six grandchildren. Susanne was bilingual in French and English. She had been an accomp
2 CONC lished horsewoman, winning the Ladies Hunters Class competition at the Southside Virginia Fair in 1939. During World War II she qualified to fly solo and became a member of the Civil Air Patrol. When she and Van Stockum met in New
2 CONC  York, she was a buyer for an exclusive clothier on Fifth Avenue.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a service wife Susanne was extremely popular in military and civilian circles. During her husband's tour of duty in Washington, D. C., she was elected president of the Washington area Marine Corps Wives' Club. With her social g
2 CONC races and French background she was a hostess renowned for her memorable dinners. Her hallmark among her friends and acquaintances was her genuine concern for the welfare of others. She touched many lives. Her beloved grandmother 
2 CONC Sue Henning had been described as "a beautiful lady and so very kind." The greatest tribute to be paid Susanne is to describe her in these very same terms.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's retirement from the Marine Corps, Susanne returned to Allen Dale with her family in 1970, restoring the historic Allen Dale residence. She was a member of the National Society of the Colonial Dames, the Filson 
2 CONC Club Historical Society, the Pendennis Club, the Shelby County Historical Society and the Alliance Francaise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Visitation will be at Shannon Funeral Home from five until eight p.m. on Tuesday, May 23. Funeral services will be held at the Church of the Annunciation at ten a.m. on Wednesday, May 24 with burial immediately following at Grove 
2 CONC Hill Cemetery.
