1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  Princess Marie Françoise of Bourbon-Parma (born August 19, 1928) is a member of the House of Bourbon. The eldest daughter of the titular Duke of Parma, she married Prince Edouard de Lobkowicz. Her family survived 
2 CONC Nazi imprisonment during World War II, and she later aided Hungarian refugees in 1956.
2 CONT
2 CONT Key facts about her life and background include:
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal Lineage: Born in Paris, her father was Prince Xavier of Bourbon-Parma, the Carlist pretender to the Spanish throne. Her mother, Madeleine de Bourbon-Busset, belonged to the senior non-dynastic Bourbon-Busset line.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early Life Challenges: During World War II, her father was arrested by the Nazis and sent to the Dachau concentration camp. She later fled to Austria during the Hungarian Revolution in 1956.
2 CONT
2 CONT Landmark Marriage: On January 7, 1960, she married Prince Edouard de Lobkowicz at Notre Dame Cathedral in Paris. It was the first marriage of a member of the House of Bourbon at Notre Dame since 1816.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family & Children: Marie Françoise and her husband had four children. She is the elder sister of Prince Carlos Hugo, the former head of the House of Bourbon-Parma, and Princess Marie-Thérèse.
2 CONT
