1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Anne Antoinette Françoise Charlotte di Borbone, Anne Antoinette Françoise Charlotte 
2 CONT *Princess(Principessa) di Bourbon-Parma.
2 CONT *Princess(prinzessin) von Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen
2 CONT *Queen consort of the Romanians.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Links:==
2 CONT *[http://thepeerage.com/p10112.htm#i101116 The Peerage] 
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/F/per_page.php?id=10067 Geneall]
2 CONT *[http://www.hansdenyngre.dk/hans_uk/wizg81.htm#1909 Johann the Younger #2738]
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_Anne_of_Romania Wikipedia]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Queen Anne of Romania was the wife of Michael I, former King of Romania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was born in Paris, France, the only daughter of Prince René of Bourbon-Parma and Princess Margaret of Denmark. With her three brothers she spent her childhood in France. Their holidays were spent alternately at the Villa Pian
2 CONC ore in Lucca with their paternal grandmother the Dowager Duchess of Parma, or at Bernstorff Palace in Copenhagen with their maternal grandfather. In 1939 her family fled from the Nazis and escaped to Spain. From there they went on
2 CONC  to Portugal and then to the United States. She attended the Parsons School of Design in New York City from 1940 to 1943. She also worked as a sales assistant at Macy's department store. In 1943, she volunteered for military servi
2 CONC ce in the French Army. She served in Algeria, Morocco, Italy, Luxembourg and in liberated Germany, as an ambulance driver. Anne received the French Croix de guerre for her wartime service.
2 CONT
2 CONT In November 1947, Anne met King Michael I of Romania who was visiting London for the wedding of Princess Elizabeth and Philip Mountbatten. In fact, a year previously Queen Mother Elena had invited Anne, her mother, and brothers fo
2 CONC r a visit to Bucharest, but the plan did not come off. Meanwhile, Michael had glimpsed Anne in a newsreel and requested a photograph from the film footage.
2 CONT
2 CONT She did not want to accompany her parents to London for the royal wedding as she wished to avoid meeting King Michael in official surroundings. Instead, she planned to stay behind, go alone to the Paris railway station and, preten
2 CONC ding to be a passerby in the crowd, privately observe the king as his entourage escorted him to his London-bound train. However, at the last moment she was persuaded by her cousin, Prince Jean of Luxembourg, to come to London, whe
2 CONC re he planned to host a party. Upon arrival, she stopped by Claridge's to see her parents, and found herself being introduced unexpectedly to King Michael. Abashed to the point of confusion, she clicked her heels instead of curtse
2 CONC ying, and fled in embarrassment. Charmed, the king saw her again the night of the wedding at the Luxembourg embassy soirée, confided in her some of his concerns about the Communist takeover of Romania and fears for his mother's sa
2 CONC fety, and nicknamed her Nan. They saw each other several times thereafter on outings in London, always chaperoned by her mother or brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT A few days later, she accepted an invitation to accompany Michael and his mother when he piloted a Beechcraft aeroplane to take his aunt Princess Irene, Duchess of Aosta, back home to Lausanne. Sixteen days after meeting, Michael 
2 CONC proposed to Anne while the couple were out on a drive in Lausanne. She initially declined, but later accepted after taking long walks and drives with him. Although Michael gave her an engagement ring a few days later, he felt obli
2 CONC ged to refrain from a public announcement until he informed his government, despite the fact that the press besieged them in anticipation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael returned to Romania, where he was told by the prime minister that a wedding announcement was not "opportune". Yet within days it was used as the government's public explanation for Michael's sudden "abdication", when in fa
2 CONC ct the king was deposed by the Communists on 30 December. Anne was unable to get further news of Michael until he left the country. They finally reunited in Davos on 23 January 1948.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a Bourbon, Anne was bound by the canon law of the Roman Catholic Church, which required that she receive a dispensation to marry a non-Catholic Christian (Michael was Orthodox). At the time, such a dispensation was normally onl
2 CONC y given if the non-Roman Catholic partner promised to allow the children of the marriage to be raised as Roman Catholics. Michael refused to make this promise since it would have violated Romania's monarchical constitution, and wo
2 CONC uld be likely to have a detrimental impact upon any possible restoration. The Holy See (which handled the matter directly since Michael was a member of a reigning dynasty) refused to grant the dispensation unless Michael made the 
2 CONC required promise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Helen, Queen Mother of Romania and her sister Princess Irene of Greece and Denmark, Duchess of Aosta (an Orthodox married to a Catholic Prince) met with the fiancée's parents in Paris, where the two families resolved to take their
2 CONC  case to the Vatican in person. In early March, the couple's mothers met with Pope Pius XII who, despite the entreaties of the Queen Mother and the fact that Princess Margrethe pounded her fist on the table in anger, refused permi
2 CONC ssion for Anne to marry Michael.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been surmised that the Pope's refusal was, in part, motivated by the fact that when Princess Giovanna of Italy married Anne's cousin, King Boris III of Bulgaria, in 1930, the couple had undertaken to raise their future chil
2 CONC dren as Roman Catholics, but had baptized them in the Orthodox faith in deference to Bulgaria's state religion. However, Michael declined to make a promise he could not keep politically, while Anne's mother was herself the daughte
2 CONC r of a mixed marriage between a Catholic (Marie d'Orléans) and a Protestant (Prince Valdemar of Denmark), who had abided by their pre-ne temere compromise to raise their sons as Protestant and their daughter, Margrethe, as Catholi
2 CONC c.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although under a great deal of stress, the engaged couple resolved to proceed. Anne's paternal uncle, Xavier, Duke of Parma, issued a statement objecting to any marriage conducted against the will of the Pope and the bride's famil
2 CONC y. It was he, not the Pontiff, who forbade Anne's parents to attend the wedding. Michael's spokesman declared on 9 June that the parents had been asked and had given their consent, and that the bride's family would be represented 
2 CONC at the nuptials by her maternal uncle, the Protestant Prince Erik of Denmark, who was to give the bride away.
2 CONT
2 CONT As no papal dispensation was given for the marriage, when it was celebrated according to the rites of the Eastern Orthodox Church, it was deemed invalid by the Roman Catholic Church, but perfectly legal by every other authority. T
2 CONC he couple would eventually take part in a religious ceremony again, on 9 November 1966, at the Roman Catholic Church of St Charles in Monaco, thus satisfying Roman Catholic canon law.
2 CONT
2 CONT After their wedding in 1948, Anne and Michael rented a house in Hertfordshire for four years, where they became market gardeners and farmed poultry. In 1956 they moved to Versoix on Lake Geneva, and raised five daughters there. In
2 CONC  1992 Anne and Michael visited Romania for three days; it was Anne's first visit to the country. From 1993 to 1997, despite repeated attempts, Michael was refused entry to Romania by the hostile Romanian government. During these y
2 CONC ears Anne visited the country a number of times representing her husband. After 1997, there were no restrictions on Anne and Michael's entry into Romania. Elisabeta Palace was put at their disposal by the government, and they reco
2 CONC vered some properties from the state, including Săvârşin Castle and Peleş Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 2008, Anne and Michael celebrated their Diamond Wedding Anniversary with 3 days of events in Romania which was the largest celebration the couple ever had together subsequent to their wedding in June 1948.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne and King Michael had five daughters, all of whom have been married and three of whom have children: Crown Princess Margareta (b. 26 March 1949), married Radu Duda on 21 September 1996. Princess Elena of Romania (b. 15 Novembe
2 CONC r 1950), married Robin Medforth-Mills on 20 July 1983 and was divorced on 28 November 1991. They have two children. She married secondly Alexander McAteer on 14 August 1998. Nicholas de Roumanie Medforth-Mills (b. 1 April 1985) El
2 CONC isabeta-Karina de Roumanie Medforth-Mills (b. 4 January 1989) Irina Walker (b. 28 February 1953); born Princess Irina of Romania, she married John Kreuger on 4 October 1983, from whom she was divorced on 24 November 2003. They hav
2 CONC e two children. She married secondly John Wesley Walker on 10 November 2007. Michael Torsten Kreuger (b. 25 February 1984), married Tara Littlefield in February 2011. Kohen Kreuger (b. March 2012) Angelica-Margareta Bianca (b. 29 
2 CONC December 1986), married Richard Robert Knight in 2009. Courtney Bianca Knight (b. 2007) Diana Knight (b. 2011) Princess Sophie (b. 29 October 1957), married Alain Michel Biarneix on 29 August 1998 and was divorced in 2002, with a 
2 CONC daughter. Elisabeth-Maria de Roumanie Biarneix (b. 15 August 1998) Princess Maria (b. 13 July 1964), married Kazimierz Wiesław Mystkowski[12] on 16 September 1995 and divorced in 2003.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was the younger sister of Prince Jacques of Bourbon-Parma and elder sister to Prince Michel of Bourbon-Parma who is married to Princess Maria Pia of Savoy (eldest child of King Umberto II of Italy and Queen Marie José), and P
2 CONC rince André of Bourbon-Parma.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a granddaughter of Robert I, Duke of Parma she was first cousin to: King Boris III of Bulgaria; Robert Hugo, Duke of Parma; Princess Alicia, Dowager Duchess of Calabria; Carlos Hugo, Duke of Parma; Crown Prince Otto of Austria;
2 CONC  and Grand Duke Jean of Luxembourg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne died on 1 August 2016 in a hospital in Morges, Switzerland, at the age of 92. Although the offer to confer a posthumous military medal on her was declined by her family, Romania's President Klaus Iohannis offered condolences 
2 CONC to King Michael and the royal family, issuing a statement which described the deceased as devoted to the country whose name she bore, "Her Majesty Queen Ana of Romania will remain forever in memory and in our hearts as one of the 
2 CONC most important symbols of wisdom, dignity and, especially, as a model of moral conduct.". The government of Romania declared that the 13 August 2016 shall be a national day of mourning, during which the Romanian flag when displaye
2 CONC d is to fly at half-mast at all institutions and buildings, private, cultural and partisan as well as public, and television and radio broadcasts are to adapt their programming appropriately in memory of Anne of Romania, whose fun
2 CONC eral will be conducted that day at the Curtea de Argeș Cathedral. Two days later, on 5 August, President Nicolae Timofti of Moldova likewise decreed national mourning on 13 August in memory of Queen Anne, also calling for the repu
2 CONC blic to observe a moment of silence at 10 am on that day.
