2 NOTE How delightful!
2 NOTE This is great!  
2 NOTE In 1927, Theodor David Erlanger traveled to New York with Prince Ernst Heinrich of Saxony as his legal advisor.  Prince Ernst, third son of the King of Saxony, was studying business practices now that his family had lost their roy
3 CONC al state after the unification of Germany.  The traveling party stayed at the Ritz Carlton, and Dr. Theodore Erlanger secured a prescription for one pint of Whiskey.  During Prohibition, obtaining liquor 'for medical purposes' was
3 CONC  one of the only legal ways to get a drink, and since Prince Ernst couldn't understand the peculiar non-alcoholic habits of Americans, my grandfather obliged!  
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/36/3e/9b/49/5344483ef6dc6a5c/prince_ernst_original.jpg?hash=6d15a333e4cf1eaaec46f7ff1aa8547cbc5c7ed2cb5a07cc64be37805f8c2d65.1797062399
2 FORM jpg
2 NOTE The Prince is on Geni.  I tagged the photo.
2 NOTE I also think that Princ Ernst is the client who gave Theo the gold Breguet watch from Napoleonic times (a valuable antique even then). I was so sad when Lilo chose to sell it rather than pass it down to us. It was very nice. Ah we
3 CONC ll, so it went. 
2 NOTE What i was told was not tthat Theo called the Prince but that the Prince placed a phone call to the camp commander, who was a royalist, and said that he needed his "Jewish lawyer" to appear in court for him. I was also told that P
3 CONC rince Ernst was a Freemason, as was Theo and certain guards at the camp and the camp commander, and that this was why they helped him. A bribe may also have exchanged hands. I was told he was in Dachau only three days. At one poin
3 CONC t in my younger years i remember seeing a typed letter or card that Theo had sent to Lilo, simply telling her not to worry, that he had gotten out, was in England, and would be in America as soon as he could get papers. I don't ha
3 CONC ve it now and have not seen it in years. No idea what happened to it. 
2 NOTE I never heard about Theo getting out of Dachau with a call to Prince Ernst.  The way we understood his release had to do with his non-Jewish law partner in Munich who vouched for him (or bribed someone).   Prince Ernst was indeed 
3 CONC the source of a crystal ash tray and a crystal decanter with his monogram on them.  He gave these momentos as Christmas presents.  I have the ash tray and the decanter.  I never heard about the salad serving set, but that makes se
3 CONC nse.  Isn't the whiskey prescription priceless??  Especially since I'm watching "Prohibition" on PBS as I write this.  
2 NOTE I believe that this is also the man whose word got Theo freed when Theo was briefly pulled in to a camp. I also think that he was the source of the fabulous salad serving set with Spanish galleons on the handles. (I think Letitia 
3 CONC has those.)
2 NOTE I will try to scan a better copy of this article so you can read it!
2 NOTE This is Prince Ernst Heinrich of Saxony, a client of my grandfather Theodor David Erlanger, J.D.  After German unification, the crown of Saxony no longer ruled so the former lands and castles, what was left (that's what my grandfa
3 CONC ther did - try to preserve as much of the royal estate as possible), was managed by the family.  Prince Ernst came to New York with my grandfather in his retinue to study business practices.  Apparently the Prince was already pret
3 CONC ty business-smart, as he had a press clipping agency clipping the many articles in major newspapers across the U.S. writing about his visit.   
