1 NOTE RULED FROM 1916-1918
1 NOTE Following the assassination of his uncle, Archduke Francis Ferdinand, and 
2 CONT the death of Francis Joseph, Charles succeeded as emperor of Austria and 
2 CONT king of Hungary. During World War I, in a secret letter, he supported the 
2 CONT claims of France against those of the Austrian ally Germany in 
2 CONT Alsace-Lorraine and proposed that Germany withdraw from Belgium. Charles 
2 CONT disavowed the letter when it was published in April 1918, but it had a 
2 CONT disheartening effect on the Central Powers. Upon the collapse of the 
2 CONT Austro-Hungarian Empire on November 11, 1918, Charles abdicated the throne 
2 CONT of Hungary. In March 1919 he left Austria, and in April the Austrian 
2 CONT parliament formally deposed him. Twice in 1921 Charles launched 
2 CONT unsuccessful attempts to regain the Hungarian throne. Banished from 
2 CONT Hungary, he went into exile on the island of Madeira. He was the last of 
2 CONT the Habsburg rulers.
1 NOTE Charles Francis Joseph (Karl Franz Josef) Emperor of Austria (1916-1918) abdicated in 1918, was formally deposed by the Austrian Parliament in 1919, lived in Switzerland from 1919-1921, made two attempts to regain the throne of Hu
2 CONC ngary, was deported by the Allies to Madeira in 1921, and died at Funchal, Portugal in 1922.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles I, also known as Charles IV was  named Charles Francis Joseph (Karl Franz Josef)
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles IV proclaimed himself King of Hungary in 1916.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles I of the Hapsburg-Austria line, succeed his great uncle Franz Josef, aka Francis Joseph I, as Emperor of Austria in 1916.  His short reign ended in 1918 when Austria was made a Republic at the end of World War I.  End of t
2 CONC he Hapsburg dynasty which began with Rudolf I in 1276, lasted 642 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the end of World War I, on 12 November 1918, Emperor Charles I of Austria abdicated, and Austria became a republic.  End of the Hapsburg Monarchy, and the Leopoldine line.
