1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infante_Ferdinand_of_Spain '''Infante Ferdinand of Spain'''] ;  '''Prince Ferdinand of the Two Sicilies''' (Spanish: '''Fernando de Borbón-Dos Sicilias y Borbón''', French: '''Ferdin
2 CONC and de Deux-Siciles'''; 6 March 1903 – 4 August 1905) was a member of the House of Bourbon-Two Sicilies and an Infante of Spain. He was the second and youngest son of Prince Carlos of Bourbon-Two Sicilies and his Spanish wife, Inf
2 CONC anta Mercedes, former Princess of Asturias. He was a brother of Infante Alfonso, future Duke of Calabria and Infanta Isabel Alfonsa, future Countess Zamoyska.
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT * Born in 1903 in Madrid, the young Infante was the cousin of the reigning monarch of Spain, Alfonso XIII of Spain. As there had been a period when his father had no male heirs, his mother was the former Princess of Asturias. Ferd
2 CONC inand was actually baptised Ferdinand Maria Anthony Alfonso Charles Frederick Igancio Olegario (Spanish: Fernando María Antonio Alfonso Carlos Federico Ignacio Olegario). Although born a Prince of the Two Sicilies, his father had 
2 CONC renounced his rights to the throne of the Two Sicilies (actually his family consisted of pretenders; the Two Sicilies were then part of the Kingdom of Italy). Thus, although a Prince of the Two Sicilies by birth and title, Ferdina
2 CONC nd was actually in line to inherit the Spanish throne, a legacy his mother left him with.
2 CONT * In 1904, his mother died while giving birth to her only daughter. After this incident, first in line for the throne was Ferdinand's brother Alfonso; although he was never made Prince of Asturias because it was expected that Alfo
2 CONC nso XIII would get married and have issue (this indeed happened, as Alfonso married Victoria Eugenie of Battenberg and had many children). [edit] Death
2 CONT * During the family vacation at the Palace of Miramar in San Sebastián, the Infante fell ill and died after a series of seizures. He was buried in the Pantheón de Infantes in El Escorial.
